A||N||7||bHooper||Buying a car at a Auction?||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||02-11-2000||08:43 PM||||Has anyone ever bought a car, fiero or other, at a auction?  There is a engine exchange place, about a hour away, having a auction tommorow.  They have 2 fiero's!  One 4 cyl and one V-6... both have new engines.  I am going to check it out, but wondered what other peoples experience's with auction's had been?  Did you get a good price?  I don't want to spend my restoration money (for my GT) on another car... but if I get a good deal on a driver, why not?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Sage||02-11-2000||09:00 PM||||All depends on who is putting the auction on.  If you don't have the scoop on the cars you are interested in ahead of time, it is pretty much a "pig in a poke" situation.  Some of them will let you start them up, I have never been to one where they will let you drive it before hand, but again it is up to the individual auction. If they allow you to come before the auction starts and check things out, it may be worth bidding if you see something that looks reasonable.  If you can't check it out before time of the auction, I might go, but would probably hang back unless something was obviously what you wanted at the right price.  Most of the "good ones" have plenty of dealers show up and they have more money to play with and usually know the auctioneers.  It is fun to go and watch either way.  Sometimes the banks have repo auctions, and you CAN find some real deals.  Like I said, all depends on who is putting it on.  If you bid, set your max before you start and if someone goes over you, best to pull out unless you REEEAAALLY want it!  Just my slant on the situation, from limited experience.  Let us know if you get something good!||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||02-11-2000||09:09 PM||||Unfortunately, I just found out about it today.  But they were very open about letting  people come and look at the cars.  According to them, they are conductiong the auction themselves.  But as this ad ran in the local Trading Post, for at least a week, I'm sure there will be some dealers there!  <P>Of course, who's to say they aren't profesional auctioneer's on the side?!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Either way, I am setting a verrry lowww limit for myself.  That way if I like the car, it does have a new engine in it, I want hurt my checkbook to easily!||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||02-11-2000||09:31 PM||||The guy I bought my car from bought it at an auction in Modesto CA. One of those huge places that advertise the drug seizure auctions. (still can't find that pound of dope <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) I have the paperwork showing that he paid $2500 plus 10% fees. Within a year he replaced clutch and master cylinder and then sold it to me for $2500.<BR>You can find a good deal on your own, but since these cars have new engines,I think they would be worth bidding on if the engine work is warranteed to the buyer.  The rebuild on my 2.8 was $2700 alone.<BR>Bid on the value of the engine work and add a percentage baseed on the condition of the car. I'm no expert, just sounds like a good formula to me.  Good Luck.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||02-11-2000||10:01 PM||||That makes sense... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||02-11-2000||10:46 PM||||Since these are all cars that were abandoned at the shp instead of paying for the work,that probably means they have a  salvaged title, right?  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||02-12-2000||08:36 AM||||Usually at auctions you make out better if there are a lot of dealers there. A dealer wants to make a profit on a car so they must get the car for less than market value||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||02-12-2000||02:46 PM||||Well, I'm back from the auction, so here's the low-down.  The 4 cyl/4 speed coupe, went for $650... with a new engine, but it smelled like the prior owner smoked 5 packs of butts a day in the car.  I only bid $400 on that car.<P>The 6 cyl, was a '86 GT!  Automatic, silver with power windows and rw defrost.  The seats were ripped, and the carpet was wearing out... it could use a paint job as well.  This car went for $1405!  I thought it was worth $1100, so that was the most that I bid on the car.  I know that the sale price even seems kinda low, but I figured it would cost another $1100 to fix it up.<P>The funny thing about the whole auction, this was a country bumpkin fest... there were no fiero people there, besides me, and the car sold for much more than many "cars of interest".  I guess what I'm saying is, I'm shocked that these ignorant people bid more on the fiero than the pick up truck, the sunbird, the Geo Strom wagon, and half as much as the 2 continentals, 2 caddy's and the suburban!<P>Just when you think people arent putting any credit into the vehicles.... ?||||reg||9
